{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1041413-martial-arts","id":1041413,"title":"Martial Arts","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-01-31T12:40:35-08:00","id":1041413,"title":"Martial Arts","content":"Does the Martial Arts Fighting Style have any basis in reality? Doing Lethal without weapons or a supernatural ability seem like something from an action movie, I think. Doing the same damage as a machete is just downright ridiculous to me. I like the other Brawl styles just fine, as they expand nicely on tactical options and seem to conform more to the type of gritty and realistic genre that I run in my games.<br /> <br /> \u200bBut then again, I know very little of weapon-less martial arts and how much damage a professional martial artist actually can do. If anyone initiated can enlighten me or even change my mind, please do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9849-aristarkos","name":"Aristarkos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9849_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"369"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T13:16:49-08:00","id":1041437,"title":"","content":"We are talking 1e or 2e?<br /> <br /> In 2e, this is simple: training yourself to do lethal damage with your hands is a mixture of physical conditioning and specialized training in how to inflict lasting damage by knowing things like how to effectively strike specific joints/bones/etc. that are significant weak points in the human body.<br /> <br /> A normal Brawler with punch you in the chest and head until eventually a rib breaks, or a facial bone snaps.<br /> <br /> Someone highly trained in Martial Arts will be striking you with the precision to reliably do things like break bones and dislocate joints with each hit.<br /> <br /> How based this is in reality... depends on how you look at these things in terms of abstraction. Martial arts training in many systems do incorporate training for debilitating strikes that are designed to deny opponents the ability to fight back in as few moves as possible. In practical combat training, you want to gain as much advantage over your opponent as fast as possible, and many of the techniques to do so do require specific training to pull off reliably. But plenty of these things also make more sense as Tilt inflicting attacks than doing lethal.<br /> <br /> So the realism... is a bit of a wash. It makes some sense for some styles, but not for others. But that's one of the reasons why Hurt Locker made Fighting Styles more flexible in which moves you can learn.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T13:27:46-08:00","id":1041442,"title":"","content":"Yes, I am talking about the specific Fighting Style Martial Arts in the Chronicles of Darkness rulebook. I'm sorry for not specifying myself.<br /> <br /> \u200bI was actually thinking the same thing; having the fourth and fifth dot in Martial Arts cause Tilts more easily rather than doing lethal. Has anyone done such a homebrew and like to share?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9849-aristarkos","name":"Aristarkos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9849_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"369"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T13:34:19-08:00","id":1041444,"title":"","content":"It's a little inaccurate to say a character with martial arts training does &quot;as much damage&quot; as a machete. A machete objectively deals more damage, it having a +2 damage modifier, where unarmed attacks do +0. The difference between bashing damage and lethal damage isn't about &quot;how much&quot; damage, but about the nature of the damage. Bashing is non-critical damage like minor bruises, scrapes, shallow cuts, etc. whereas lethal is life threatening damage like major contusions, broken bones, deep gashes, etc. I'm not a martial arts expert either, but it seems pretty reasonable to me that someone with martial arts training would know how to strike to break bones, dislocate joints, cause concussions, or otherwise cause life-threatening harm. In terms of quantity, it's more comparable to an average person punching with a tool like a cestus, push knife, or brass knuckles.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T14:02:23-08:00","id":1041467,"title":"","content":"Stacking FS: MA 4 and 5 nets you lethal damage and a +2 damage modifier, which does make your hands as dangerous as a machete.<br /> <br /> -----<br /> <br /> As for homebrewing things, the Hurt Locker expansions to Fighting Styles largely negates the need to homebrew much. I'm not sure if the specific Tilt inflicting options are in there off the top of my head, but the expanded system in Hurt Locker allows you to swap maneuvers if they're of the appropriate types.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T14:05:35-08:00","id":1041469,"title":"","content":"Basically at that point you're dealing with someone whose 'hands are registered weapons,' so to speak. It isn't a schoolyard fight, it's the ability to inflict serious damage.<br /> <br /> But it's important to recognize that each attack is not a punch or a kick, rolls can represent series of attacks, so it can be a series of powerful blows to the ribs and so forth.<br /> <br /> Look at professional fighters like Mike Tyson.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QpdJFFBMYNs\" data-code=\"QpdJFFBMYNs\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/QpdJFFBMYNs/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> Or Tommy 'Hitman' Hearns.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HEXlSCuw5KA\" data-code=\"HEXlSCuw5KA\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/HEXlSCuw5KA/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> Both were fantastic at knocking out opponents, and that's 'your brain is slammed against the inside of your skull' kind of damage, like someone in a speeding car not wearing a seatbelt suddenly driving into a wall.<br /> <br /> You can see here other damage, though it's more graphic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=x4h2ysQEblQ\" data-code=\"x4h2ysQEblQ\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/x4h2ysQEblQ/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> And a lot of that is due to friction, sure, but they're still things that happen in fights. Imagine if these fights were going on in a street, without a referee, without the safety measures they already have, such as boxing gloves and pads. <a href=\"https://www.thrillist.com/culture/how-hard-could-mike-tyson-punch-measuring-punching-force-and-equivalents\" target=\"_blank\">This is all very serious damage</a>, it's just more spread out than you would get from a machete or knife, which allows all the force to be directed from, ideally, the edge of the blade.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 01-31-2017, 05:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T14:45:48-08:00","id":1041490,"title":"","content":"Hello there, I'm your friendly neighbourhood Martial Arts student! I've been practicing for about eleven years now, and in this time the question has come up a few times, especially when my teacher would bring in some of his teachers, who were generally focused a little more on practicality than on exercise value. Out of curiosity, do you guys know how much force, properly applied, it takes to break someone's rib? According to a study by <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cynthia_Bir\" target=\"_blank\">Cynthia Bir</a> in 2010 a force of 3,300 Newtons has a 25% chance of breaking the average person's rib. The comparable force for cracking a femur is 4,000 Newtons. The same study calculated that a trained boxer hits with 5,000 Newtons of force with their hands, and a trained martial artist kicking hits with 9,000 Newtons.<br /> <br /> This means that a boxer stands a 38% chance of breaking someone's rib with their bare hands, and a martial artist stands a 68% chance of breaking a rib with their bare feet.<br /> <br /> Now, keep in mind that this is targeting the bone <i>directly</i> instead of going for weaker spots like the knee, or the tibia and fibula. There's a common story spread amongst martial arts clubs that it takes only about forty pounds to the side of the leg to break the knee, and I have no idea how true that is or not, because I have never been, and hope to never be, in a situation where I would need to actually try and destroy a person's knee. That said, when you learn more practical applications, you learn a few tricks, such as being able to hit a person more than once with one movement. We had an instructor show the advanced students, a few years back, what he called the Buzzsaw Technique, which mostly involved following through with a punch. It goes something like this:<br /> <br /> 1) Punch 2) Follow through and strike with elbow 3) Hit with elbow again as you recoil 4) Smash with knuckles again as your hand passes back across their body<br /> <br /> While with your forwards step you also...<br /> <br /> 1) Kick their shin 2) Stomp on their foot 3) Smash your shin into the side of their knee, potentially spraining or breaking it<br /> <br /> This is one move that took this master about a second to complete. In the equivalent of a CofD turn (three seconds) he's punched you using that follow through motion at least twice more and has probably kneed you in the groin. Knowing this master, he has probably also found a way to rip at your skin with your bare hands with a Kino Mutai technique. Which is, coincidentally, also a Merit in Hurt Locker, I think. In total, in one CofD turn, he's hit you about fifteen times. With enough force to have close to a 50/50 chance of breaking your ribs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1127694-arcanist-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\"><b>My Homebrew Hub</b></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4152-arcanist","name":"Arcanist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T15:40:31-08:00","id":1041527,"title":"","content":"Ouch, well now I have something to imagine when a Brawl 5 MA 5 Area of Expertise rolls an Exceptional Success. Thanks for that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4714-live-bait","name":"Live Bait","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"703"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T15:55:56-08:00","id":1041538,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Arcanist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1041413-martial-arts?p=1041490#post1041490\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hello there, I'm your friendly neighbourhood Martial Arts student! I've been practicing for about eleven years now, and in this time the question has come up a few times, especially when my teacher would bring in some of his teachers, who were generally focused a little more on practicality than on exercise value. Out of curiosity, do you guys know how much force, properly applied, it takes to break someone's rib? According to a study by <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cynthia_Bir\" target=\"_blank\">Cynthia Bir</a> in 2010 a force of 3,300 Newtons has a 25% chance of breaking the average person's rib. The comparable force for cracking a femur is 4,000 Newtons. The same study calculated that a trained boxer hits with 5,000 Newtons of force with their hands, and a trained martial artist kicking hits with 9,000 Newtons.<br /> <br /> This means that a boxer stands a 38% chance of breaking someone's rib with their bare hands, and a martial artist stands a 68% chance of breaking a rib with their bare feet.<br /> <br /> Now, keep in mind that this is targeting the bone <i>directly</i> instead of going for weaker spots like the knee, or the tibia and fibula. There's a common story spread amongst martial arts clubs that it takes only about forty pounds to the side of the leg to break the knee, and I have no idea how true that is or not, because I have never been, and hope to never be, in a situation where I would need to actually try and destroy a person's knee. That said, when you learn more practical applications, you learn a few tricks, such as being able to hit a person more than once with one movement. We had an instructor show the advanced students, a few years back, what he called the Buzzsaw Technique, which mostly involved following through with a punch. It goes something like this:<br /> <br /> 1) Punch 2) Follow through and strike with elbow 3) Hit with elbow again as you recoil 4) Smash with knuckles again as your hand passes back across their body<br /> <br /> While with your forwards step you also...<br /> <br /> 1) Kick their shin 2) Stomp on their foot 3) Smash your shin into the side of their knee, potentially spraining or breaking it<br /> <br /> This is one move that took this master about a second to complete. In the equivalent of a CofD turn (three seconds) he's punched you using that follow through motion at least twice more and has probably kneed you in the groin. Knowing this master, he has probably also found a way to rip at your skin with your bare hands with a Kino Mutai technique. Which is, coincidentally, also a Merit in Hurt Locker, I think. In total, in one CofD turn, he's hit you about fifteen times. With enough force to have close to a 50/50 chance of breaking your ribs.</div> </div> </div> </div>And now I have something to show to people who discredit real-life martial arts way too much, born out of distate for all those supernatural martial arts we see in fiction.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>MtAw Homebrew:<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1051456-even-more-legacies-updated-to-2e#post1051456\" target=\"_blank\">Even more Legacies, updated to 2E</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1443819-new-2e-legacies-expanded\" target=\"_blank\">New 2E Legacies, expanded</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12619-21c-hermit","name":"21C Hermit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12619_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3489"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-31T16:13:06-08:00","id":1041552,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>21C Hermit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1041413-martial-arts?p=1041538#post1041538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And now I have something to show to people who discredit real-life martial arts way too much, born out of distate for all those supernatural martial arts we see in fiction.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bI tended myself towards this group of people, regarding it as a sport and showmanship rather than actually useful fighting techniques.<br /> \u200b<br /> Well, I am no more. Thank you for your demonstrations and explanations!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9849-aristarkos","name":"Aristarkos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9849_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"369"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}